0..76 Comment "// A loop drives an endpoint through a recursive protocol, one pass per turn"
77..96 Comment "// of the protocol."
97..99 Comment "//"
100..172 Comment "// A loop\'s passes are the passes of one fixpoint, and an endpoint bound"
173..249 Comment "// outside it would be captured by every pass. The loop threads the endpoint"
250..288 Comment "// instead, as it threads a held slot."
289..291 Comment "//"
292..371 Comment "// The lowering hands the endpoint to the loop\'s first pass and places it again"
372..449 Comment "// in each pass. The first pass takes it at the state the loop starts in, the"
450..526 Comment "// loop head\'s state, and `continue` hands it on at that state again. A loop"
527..600 Comment "// whose every pass acts on its endpoint before it runs again follows its"
601..638 Comment "// protocol, so it owes no `Partial`."
639..641 Comment "//"
642..657 Comment "// - see: #4502"
658..673 Comment "// - see: #4705"
675..749 Comment "/// A desk following a seat\'s reports until it retires: each report is one"
750..819 Comment "/// pass, and the retirement closes the endpoint and leaves the loop."
